{"name":"Sodium/potassium manipulation (peak week)","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/Sodium_Atom.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sodium_Atom.png","caption":"Sodium Atom","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":21,"casual":{"lead":["Peak week is the final stretch of bodybuilding contest preparation, in which competitors adjust sodium, potassium, carbohydrate and water intake to look drier and more defined on stage. Some appearance-focused people borrow the idea for photo shoots.","The chain: changing electrolytes and fluids is said to shift how much water sits under the skin and in muscle, while carbohydrate loading in the final stage refills glycogen, which holds water, to make muscles look full. Reference sources describe the general approach only briefly, and no controlled study of the sodium and potassium part was found."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["It is done in the last days before the event, as the final phase of a long cut, with carbohydrate, salt and fluid intake changed on a planned schedule. People use it for a specific date, not as an everyday routine, and the visible change is expected on the day itself."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Intentional dehydration is among the practices linked in reference material to higher death rates among bodybuilders. Dehydration of even a few percent of body water can impair thinking and cause dizziness, and larger losses are dangerous. Electrolyte shifts carry their own health risks."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/Sodium_Atom.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/Sodium_Atom.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","tit