.519965/"},{"title":"for me sodium is the devil for bloat","url":"https://looksmax.org/threads/for-me-sodium-is-the-devil-for-bloat.356495/","site":"looksmax.org","archived":"https://web.archive.org/web/20210606090816/https://looksmax.org/threads/for-me-sodium-is-the-devil-for-bloat.356495/"},{"title":"gunna up my asin to 12 5mg ed and cut out all sodium","url":"https://looksmax.org/threads/gunna-up-my-asin-to-12-5mg-ed-and-cut-out-all-sodium.1351/","site":"looksmax.org","archived":"https://web.archive.org/web/20220524131313/https://looksmax.org/threads/gunna-up-my-asin-to-12-5mg-ed-and-cut-out-all-sodium.1351/"}],"news":[],"social":[{"title":"YSK chicken sold in supermarkets are often \"plumped\" with a salt water solution that increases sell weight by up to 30%. A serving can contain up to 500mg of sodium, or 7-10 times what is natural.","url":"https://lemmy.world/post/49755777","site":"Lemmy · youshouldknow","score":454,"comments":191,"year":2026},{"title":"Lowering sodium intake doesn’t help heart patients","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=31081529","site":"Hacker News","score":251,"comments":110,"year":2022},{"title":"Sodium rutin extends lifespan and health span in mice by 10%","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=27347818","site":"Hacker News","score":159,"comments":120,"year":2021}],"web":[{"title":"Bodybuilding","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bodybuilding"},{"title":"Dehydration","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dehydration"},{"title":"Glycogen","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glycogen"},{"title":"Carbohydrate loading","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carbohydrate_loading"},{"title":"Edema","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edema"}]},"angle":{"goal":"Photos & framing","chain":["Adjusting salt, potassium and water","Shifts in water under the skin","Drier, more defined look on the day"],"effect":"claimed","text":"Bodybuilders adjust sodium, potassium and fluids in the final days before a show or shoot to look drier and more defined, by shifting water under the skin. It is a documented part of contest prep, and practitioners report the visual effect; controlled studies of it are limited."},"wikipedia":null,"grokipedia":null,"lead":["Sodium and potassium manipulation during peak week refers to deliberate changes to salt, potassium and fluid intake in the days before a bodybuilding contest or photo shoot, with the stated aim of a drier, more defined look. It is part of the final contest-prep stage, which reference sources describe as combining carbohydrate loading with dehydration [1].","The wiki found no controlled studies of the sodium or potassium protocols themselves. Reference material notes that intentional dehydration is among practices associated with elevated mortality among bodybuilders [1], and medical sources describe the effects of dehydration [2]."],"infobox":[["Type","Contest-prep practice (lifestyle)"],["Timeframe","Final days before a show or shoot"],["Origin","Competitive bodybuilding"],["Controlled trials","None found"]],"sections":[{"h":"Description","paras":["Competitors typically spend 12 to 14 weeks cutting, then in the final stage combine carbohydrate loading with dehydration to sharpen muscle definition and vascularity [1]. Sodium and potassium changes are one set of tactics within that final stage. Specific protocols are not described here."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Proposed mechanism","paras":["Proponents say fluid distribution can be shifted by changing electrolyte and water intake. Glycogen is stored with water [3], and carbohydrate loading is documented to cause water retention [4]. Medical sources note that sodium retention drives swelling in some conditions and that kidneys regulate water and sodium [5]."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Reported risks","paras":["Dehydration causes thirst, headache and fatigue, and a loss of 1 to 2 percent of body water can impair thinking; losses of 5 to 8 percent can cause fatigue and dizziness, and more severe losses can be fatal [2]. Re